PHASE 2 -- rl, photons=100, 29 specs, alpha=0.1, 5 seeds

1. Does the conclusion depend on the error metric?

   shift axis                    coverage (1-SSIM)    coverage (rel l2)   n
   none (in-distribution)           0.899+/-0.014        0.894+/-0.006    1015
   operator shift (Zernike)         0.755+/-0.024        0.614+/-0.023    280
   operator shift (physical)        0.922+/-0.014        0.903+/-0.009    420
   content shift                    0.495+/-0.023        0.022+/-0.003    1566
   combined                         0.015+/-0.007        0.000+/-0.000    432

   largest coverage difference between metrics: 0.473
   The two metrics are different functions of the reconstruction, so
   exact agreement is not expected. What matters is whether the
   ordering and the qualitative failure survive the swap.

   Spearman rho(1-SSIM, rel l2) on the test split: +0.903

2. Do the physically-motivated families behave like the Zernike ladder?

   family        kind      rung       |mismatch|    error  cov(SSIM)  cov(l2)   n
   defocus       zernike   extreme        0.9773   0.6430      0.657    0.514   35
   astigmatism   zernike   extreme        0.8915   0.5777      0.543    0.571   35
   coma          zernike   extreme        0.9674   0.6290      0.486    0.343   35
   spherical     zernike   extreme        0.9250   0.6251      0.800    0.514   35
   na            physical  p2             0.0742   0.1230      0.943    0.971   35
   wavelength    physical  p2             0.0547   0.1396      0.914    0.914   35
   focus_drift   physical  p4             0.6814   0.4186      0.886    0.629   35

   Zernike  families: coverage 0.486-0.800, |mismatch| 0.8915-0.9773
   physical families: coverage 0.886-0.943, |mismatch| 0.0547-0.6814

   Read these together with the magnitude column. A physical family
   that retains coverage while producing a much smaller kernel
   perturbation has not disproved the mechanism -- it has placed
   realistic parameter uncertainty lower on the same axis.

